# A str method that returns a CONTAINER, subscripted immediately, on a VARIABLE
# receiver: `s.split(",")[1]`.
#
# The variable-receiver route cleared the result's class identity
# (`recName := REC_NONE`) after calling PyParseStrMethod, so a following
# subscript had no class to resolve against and the AN_CALL reached IR lowering
# unlowered — a hard `IR_UNSUPPORTED: frontend could not lower AST node (kind 8)`
# (bug-n-nilpy-subscript-of-split-on-a-variable-receiver).
#
# The neighbouring forms all worked, which is what made it narrow: a LITERAL
# receiver, the two-step `p = s.split(","); p[1]`, `g()[1]`, `xs.copy()[0]` and
# `s.upper()[0]` were all fine. Only variable-receiver + container-returning
# method + immediate subscript failed. `parts = line.split(",")` then indexing is
# the most common line of string-handling Python there is, and the one-liner is
# what people write first.
#
# Expectation is CPython's own output.

s = "Hello,World"
print(s.split(",")[1])
print(s.split(",")[0])
line = "a:b:c"
print(line.split(":")[2])
print(line.split(":"))
p = line.split(":")
print(p[1])
print("x,y".split(",")[1])
print(s.upper()[0])
print(s.lower())
w = "  pad  "
print(w.strip().upper())
print("a-b".partition("-")[2])
print(s.encode()[0])
words = "one two three"
print(words.split()[1])
